from pathlib import Path
import sys
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('9');add=A.add
add('2.10.1-01','Energy sources supply energy by different physical processes.','Chemical reactions rearrange atoms; fission splits heavy nuclei, while fusion in the Sun joins light nuclei.','Distinguish the source from the device that converts its energy. Falling water and wind can turn turbines linked to generators; solar radiation can heat a surface or produce electrical output in a photovoltaic cell. Geothermal systems draw on Earth’s internal energy. Wave motion and tidal flow offer different water-based supplies. Follow each energy pathway rather than calling electricity a primary fuel.','Explain how a solar panel and a wind turbine can both supply electricity through different pathways.','Combine the pathways: a photovoltaic panel converts radiation directly to electrical energy.~Wind turns a rotor and generator, linking air motion to electrical output.',heading='Energy-source pathways')
add('2.10.1-01-M01','Fuel stores chemical energy.','A fuel reaction rearranges atoms and can release energy to the surroundings.','Combustion transfers energy from a fuel-and-oxygen system, often heating water to drive a turbine. The atoms are rearranged into products rather than converted wholesale into energy. Fossil fuels such as coal, oil and gas are finite; biomass can be renewable when regrowth replaces consumption.','State the energy store associated with an unburnt fuel.','A chemical energy store.')
add('2.10.1-01-M02','Water can provide energy through falling from a height, wave motion or tidal movement.','Hydroelectricity typically converts raised water’s gravitational energy through turbine motion to electrical energy.','A dam maintains a height difference that makes a flow through turbines useful. Waves are mainly driven by winds over water. Tides arise from gravitational interactions involving the Moon and Sun, so their regular rise and fall differ from weather-driven waves. Appropriate coasts or height differences are needed.','State the initial energy store exploited by water falling through a hydroelectric station.','Gravitational potential energy of the raised water.')
add('2.10.1-01-M03','Geothermal energy comes from Earth’s hot interior.','A suitable system can transfer this energy to water or steam for heating or generation.','Accessible hot rocks can heat circulating water. Steam may drive a turbine connected to a generator. Suitability depends on local geology and drilling costs, so a renewable source is not equally practical at every location.','State where geothermal energy originates.','Earth’s internal thermal energy.')
add('2.10.1-01-M04','Fission of heavy nuclei can release energy in a nuclear reactor.','The resulting heating can drive a steam-turbine generator.','Fuel supply is finite. Controlling the chain reaction and removing heat are essential engineering tasks. Radioactive products require containment and management; a low operational carbon output does not remove those concerns. This source account concerns conversion and consequences, without isotope equations.','State the nuclear process used in a conventional nuclear power reactor.','Fission of heavy nuclei.')
add('2.10.1-01-M05','Solar radiation can provide heating or photovoltaic electrical output.','The Sun’s energy is supplied by fusion of light nuclei in its interior.','A dark collector absorbs radiation and transfers energy to water in a solar thermal system. A photovoltaic cell instead produces electrical output when illuminated. These are different conversions: a water heater is not a photovoltaic panel merely because both use sunlight.','State how a photovoltaic panel’s output differs from a solar water heater’s useful output.','The panel supplies electrical energy; the water heater supplies thermal energy.')
add('2.10.1-01-M06','Wind energy uses the kinetic energy of moving air.','A wind turbine turns this motion into rotational motion and then electrical output through a generator.','Output depends strongly on wind conditions and site. A wind-driven mechanical pump can transfer energy to water without first generating electricity. Identify the actual arrangement rather than assuming every rotating wind machine has the same output.','State the energy form entering a wind turbine from the air.','Kinetic energy of the moving air.')
add('2.10.1-02','An energy-source decision combines supply, environmental effects and practical requirements.','No source choice can be justified by a renewable label alone.','Compare a remote pump with an evening urban demand. Solar equipment may suit daytime pumping, but evening supply needs storage or another source. A fossil-fuel generator can operate on demand while creating combustion emissions and consuming finite fuel. Hydroelectric schemes require suitable water and terrain and can alter habitats. State the criteria for the actual task.','Explain why solar energy may suit daytime pumping yet need an additional arrangement for reliable night supply.','Link sunlight to the use: daytime pumping can coincide with available radiation.~For night use, storage or another supply is required because illumination is absent.')
add('2.10.1-02-M01','A finite energy resource is consumed faster than natural processes replace it on human timescales.','A renewable flow or resource is replenished naturally on a relevant timescale.','Coal and oil stores are finite, and nuclear fuel is also non-renewable in this classification. Solar, wind and replenished hydroelectric supplies are renewable. Renewable does not mean unlimited power at one instant; drought, darkness or calm weather can restrict output.','Explain the distinction between finite and renewable energy sources.','Finite supplies are depleted without comparable replacement on human timescales.~Renewable supplies are naturally replenished on a relevant timescale.')
add('2.10.1-02-M02','Energy sources have different environmental effects across construction, operation and disposal.','Operational emissions alone are not a complete account of environmental impact.','Fossil combustion releases carbon dioxide and may release air pollutants. Nuclear generation produces radioactive waste requiring management. Wind and solar avoid fuel combustion during generation but involve materials, land and eventual disposal. Reservoirs can flood land and change river ecology. A useful comparison names a particular impact for each source rather than saying a source has no environmental effect.','Explain one environmental difference between coal generation and wind generation.','Coal combustion releases carbon dioxide and combustion pollutants.~Wind generation avoids that combustion during operation, although its construction and land use still have impacts.')
add('2.10.1-02-M03','Reliability depends on whether output can meet demand when needed.','Site, storage, transmission and maintenance affect the suitability of a source.','Tidal timing is predictable but output varies during a cycle. Wind and solar vary with weather and daylight. Stored fuel can support dispatchable generation if fuel and maintenance are available. Hydroelectric output depends on water resources. A remote site may favour a local system that avoids a long transmission line.','Explain why a predictable tidal supply may still require storage or another supply.','Its timing is predictable, whereas its power varies through the tidal cycle.~Demand may occur when output is low, requiring an additional supply arrangement.')
add('2.10.1-03','Nuclear fuel can release energy through controlled fission, heating a working fluid.','A turbine and generator convert part of that energy into electrical output, with other energy transferred to the surroundings.','A reactor is an energy-conversion system, not a device that produces electricity directly from a nucleus into the grid. Heat removal, containment, radiation protection, long-lived waste management and eventual decommissioning are important difficulties. Compare these with the benefit of substantial generation without fuel combustion at the station. Do not confuse reactor fission with fusion in the Sun.','Explain how energy released in a fission reactor reaches a generator’s electrical output.','Fission heats a working fluid, producing steam that turns a turbine.~The turbine drives a generator, converting mechanical input into electrical output.')
add('2.10.2-01','Renewable systems can supply electrical or thermal energy to meet different end uses.','Matching the conversion pathway to the intended service reduces unnecessary conversions.','Solar resources offer strong opportunities in Namibia, but actual output depends on location, weather, shading and equipment. The 2018 syllabus gives a world irradiance ranking; that dated ranking is not used here as a current comparison. Heating water directly may avoid generating electricity merely to heat it again. A pump or refrigerator needs the corresponding mechanical or electrical conversion.','Explain why the same sunlight resource can support a water heater and an electric water pump through different systems.','Link the service to the conversion: a collector directly heats water.~A photovoltaic panel supplies electrical energy for a pump motor.',heading='Matching renewable systems to the service')
add('2.10.2-01-M01','Renewable electricity generation requires a conversion device suited to the source.','Photovoltaic cells use radiation directly, while wind and hydroelectric machines commonly drive generators mechanically.','A small system may include a controller, storage battery and inverter where AC is required. Storage shifts the use of energy to a later time; it is not an additional primary source. Allow for conversion losses and actual demand.','Explain how a photovoltaic system can power a light after sunset.','Illumination during the day supplies electrical energy that charges a battery.~The battery later supplies the light when the panel has little output.')
add('2.10.2-01-M02','Solar collectors can heat water, and transparent greenhouse covers admit useful solar radiation.','Absorption raises internal energy, while reduced heat loss helps maintain a warmer interior.','A solar heater uses an absorber, water path and insulated storage. A greenhouse admits radiation that warms surfaces; its enclosure reduces loss by moving warm air away. Ventilation may be needed to prevent overheating. These systems use thermal pathways rather than requiring photovoltaic conversion first.','Explain why a solar water-heating tank is insulated.','The water has gained internal energy by absorbing solar input.~Insulation reduces the rate of energy transfer to cooler surroundings, retaining useful heat.')
add('2.10.2-01-M03','Renewable energy can operate a water pump through mechanical or electrical conversion.','A wind rotor may drive a pump directly, while a photovoltaic system can drive an electric motor.','Pumping raises water’s gravitational potential energy. A storage tank can retain the benefit of pumping when sunshine or wind is available, reducing the need to store electricity for later pumping. The required lifting height and water flow influence system size.','Explain how a raised storage tank helps a solar pumping system meet evening water demand.','Sunlight powers pumping into the tank during the day.~Stored gravitational potential energy then permits later water delivery without simultaneous solar input.')
add('2.10.2-01-M04','Renewable electricity can drive a refrigerator or air-conditioner’s compressor.','A refrigerator transfers energy from a cooler region to a warmer region using an external energy input.','Cooling does not mean a panel emits coldness. The machine removes thermal energy from the intended region and rejects that energy plus its input energy elsewhere. Solar electricity can supply the compressor; an appropriately designed thermal absorption system can instead use heat input. Output variability and storage must match the cooling duty.','Explain how a solar-powered refrigerator cools its contents.','Photovoltaic output supplies the energy input needed to operate the cooling machine.~The machine transfers thermal energy from the contents to the warmer surroundings.')
add('2.10.2-02','Basic renewable systems contain a collector or rotor and a useful energy-output path.','Tracking input, intermediate transfer and output distinguishes PV, water-heating and wind systems.','A PV panel supplies electrical output, a thermal collector raises water temperature, and a wind rotor supplies shaft motion that may drive a generator or pump. Diagrams should label the useful output and losses to the surroundings. A generator and an inverter perform different jobs: the former converts mechanical input, while the latter changes the electrical supply form.','Explain how the useful outputs of PV, solar water heating and a wind generator differ.','Combine the pathways: PV gives electrical output and a thermal collector gives heated water.~A wind generator links air motion through shaft rotation to electrical output.')
add('2.10.2-02-M01','A basic PV system includes illuminated cells connected to an electrical load.','A controller and battery can be included for managed storage, with an inverter when an AC load requires it.','Draw energy flow from radiation to electrical output, then to the intended device. Shade, orientation and illumination affect output. A battery adds a chemical storage stage; it must not be depicted as creating energy independently.','Describe the energy conversion in a basic illuminated PV panel connected to a lamp.','The cells convert incident radiation to electrical output that supplies the lamp.')
add('2.10.2-02-M02','A solar water heater uses an absorber to transfer incident radiation to water.','An insulated tank limits the loss of the heated water’s internal energy.','A dark collector with pipes and a transparent cover can improve useful collection. Water may circulate by a pump or, in a suitable arrangement, by density-driven convection. In a short steady-input test, temperature rise per time measures a heating rate rather than efficiency unless input and useful energy are also known.','Describe the energy path in a basic solar water heater.','Radiation is absorbed by the collector and transferred to the water, raising its internal energy.')
add('2.10.2-02-M03','A basic wind generator has a rotor coupled to a generator.','It converts some air-motion energy through rotational mechanical energy into electrical output.','Wind pumps may instead transmit shaft motion directly to a pumping mechanism. Braking, support and control protect a real machine under changing winds. The rotational stage matters: wind does not directly charge a battery without conversion equipment.','Describe the conversion stages in a basic wind generator.','Moving air turns a rotor, which drives a generator to supply electrical output.')
A.deviations.append(dict(from_='Dated Namibia irradiance ranking in the 2018 objective heading',to='Strong solar potential taught without asserting a current world rank.',why='No current ranking is needed for these conversion tasks; the dated source is identified.'))
C=A.choice
C('2.10.1-01-M01','What changes in a chemical fuel reaction?',('Atoms are rearranged','The chemical products have a different arrangement.'),('Heavy nuclei split as its defining process','That describes fission.'),('Light nuclei fuse as its defining process','That describes fusion.'),('Atoms vanish without products','That fails to conserve matter in this model.'))
C('2.10.1-01-M03','Which source uses hot rocks beneath Earth’s surface?',('Geothermal','It draws on internal thermal energy.'),('Tidal','This uses tidal movement.'),('Wind','This uses moving air.'),('Photovoltaic','This uses incident radiation.'))
C('2.10.1-01-M04','Which fuel is finite in the syllabus classification?',('Nuclear fuel','Its usable material is a finite resource.'),('Sunlight','It is treated as renewable.'),('Wind','This is a replenished flow.'),('Tidal flow','This is renewable on human timescales.'))
C('2.10.1-01-M05','Which process supplies the Sun’s energy?',('Fusion','Light nuclei join in the Sun.'),('Coal combustion','The Sun is not a coal furnace.'),('Hydroelectric flow','This is a water-based terrestrial system.'),('Fission of reactor fuel','That is the conventional reactor pathway.'))
C('2.10.2-02-M01','Which device converts suitable DC output to AC for a load?',('Inverter','It changes the electrical supply form.'),('Water tank','This stores water rather than changing electrical supply.'),('Turbine','It provides mechanical rotation.'),('Thermal insulation','It reduces heat transfer.'))
C('2.10.2-02-M02','What is the main useful output of a solar water heater?',('Increased water internal energy','The intended service is heating.'),('Electricity from its pipes','Pipes are not photovoltaic cells.'),('Nuclear fuel','There is no nuclear conversion here.'),('A stronger wind','Wind is not the intended output.'))
C('2.10.1-03','Which difficulty is particularly associated with fission products?',('Radioactive waste management','Some products need containment over long periods.'),('Need for a sunny night','Reactor generation does not require sunlight.'),('Dependence on an ocean tide','This is not its energy source.'),('No need to remove waste heat','Cooling remains necessary.'))
C('2.10.2-01-M03','A farm needs water after dark. Which arrangement stores daytime solar pumping’s useful result?',('A raised water tank','The stored water can later flow down.'),('A disconnected empty wire','This stores no useful pumping output.'),('A smaller unshaded panel without storage','It still lacks night illumination.'),('Removing the pump','This removes the lifting mechanism.'))
C('2.10.1-02-M03','Which consideration matters for a hydroelectric site?',('Available flow and height difference','Both affect usable water-energy input.'),('Panel-cell colour alone','This is not a hydroelectric criterion.'),('Radioactive half-life of water','This is not the relevant energy source.'),('Having no water','Flow is required for generation.'))
C('2.10.2-01-M04','A PV refrigerator is operating. Where is energy rejected?',('To the surroundings through the hot side','The removed heat plus input energy must leave.'),('Nowhere because cooling destroys energy','Energy is conserved.'),('Into the panel as manufactured sunlight','That is not the cooling pathway.'),('Into a disappearing mass','The cooling model does not remove material.'))
C('2.10.1-02-M02','A wind project has no fuel combustion during generation. What follows?',('Its operational combustion emissions are avoided','This is the supported claim.'),('Its construction has zero impact','Materials and construction can have impacts.'),('Its output cannot vary','Wind changes.'),('Its blades need no maintenance','A moving machine still needs maintenance.'))
C('2.10.2-02-M02','Water rises from 22 to 30 °C in 4 min. What is its average heating rate?',('2 °C/min','The rise is 8 °C over 4 min.'),('7.5 °C/min','This divides final temperature by time.'),('8 °C/min','This omits division by the interval.'),('32 °C/min','This multiplies rise by time.'))
P=A.part
P('2.10.1-01-M06','State the energy form carried by wind.','Kinetic energy.',1,'One mark for kinetic energy.')
P('2.10.2-02-M01','State the useful energy output of a PV panel.','Electrical energy.',1,'One mark for electrical output.')
P('2.10.2-02-M02','Explain how insulation helps a solar water tank.','It reduces energy transfer from hotter water to cooler surroundings, retaining more of the collected energy.',2,'One mark for reduced transfer; one for retaining useful internal energy.')
P('2.10.2-02-M02','Calculate the average heating rate when water warms by 9 °C in 6 min.','Rate = temperature rise/time = 1.5 °C/min.',2,'One mark for dividing rise by time; one for 1.5 °C/min.')
P('2.10.2-01-M03','Explain why storing water can suit a pump with variable solar output.','Water can be raised while sunlight is available. The tank then supplies water later without the pump operating at that instant.',2,'One mark for collecting the useful result during supply; one for later delivery independent of instantaneous output.')
A.practical=dict(graph='Plot temperature vertically against elapsed time horizontally over the short steady-input interval; fit a rising line.',unit='°C/min',meaning='The gradient is the temperature increase per minute over this interval, not an energy efficiency. A steady rate is an approximation over the stated short interval.',method=['Put a measured mass of water in a small solar collector and record its initial temperature.','Fix the collector angle and expose it to steady illumination.','Start a timer and stir gently before each temperature reading.','Record temperature at regular intervals using the same immersed thermometer position.','Repeat with the same water mass and comparable illumination, stopping before unsafe temperatures.'],control='Hold collector area, angle and water mass fixed; minimise draught changes, avoid looking into intense lamps and handle warm water carefully.',table='Record elapsed time / min and water temperature / °C; add temperature rise / °C if comparing runs.',conclusion='The temperature-time gradient compares heating rates under the controlled conditions; efficiency would additionally need input and useful energy measurements.')
if __name__=='__main__':A.build()
